---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/deliverability';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/deliverability');
const desc = "Keep a sender inventory, distinguish acceptance from inbox placement and investigate the specific stream that changed before increasing volume.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does accepted delivery mean inbox placement?",
    "answer": "No. Acceptance is a receiving-server outcome. Placement requires different evidence, and seed samples do not represent every recipient."
  },
  {
    "question": "How long should recovery take?",
    "answer": "There is no universal deadline. Resume based on the resolved cause, verified configuration and current feedback, with an owner able to stop the affected stream."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I change platforms to fix delivery?",
    "answer": "First diagnose the failure. A move does not remove permission problems or suppressed contacts and adds configuration work. Require evidence that the candidate addresses the confirmed cause."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Shopify operating playbook</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Deliverability operations: diagnose, contain and verify</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Keep a sender inventory, distinguish acceptance from inbox placement and investigate the specific stream that changed before increasing volume.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Map every sending stream</h2>
      <p>List order confirmations, shipping notices, support messages, campaigns and lifecycle promotions. Record the sending service, visible From domain, authentication domains, IP arrangement, owner and pause control for each. A merchant may have several active senders after an app migration.</p>
      <p>Separate transactional purpose from promotional purpose in the inventory and audience rules. Subdomains can help operational organization, but do not assume separation guarantees reputation isolation or that unifying providers protects order messages. Make the architecture decision with the actual sending provider and domain owner.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Verify authentication from received messages</h2>
      <p>Use a real received sample from each stream to inspect SPF, DKIM and DMARC results and alignment with the visible From domain. Compare it with the provider’s setup instructions and current DNS. A dashboard showing a verified domain is not a substitute for checking what a message actually used.</p>
      <p>Google’s <a class="link" href="https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126">Gmail sender guidelines</a> distinguish all senders from those sending more than 5,000 messages daily to personal Gmail accounts. The latter need SPF, DKIM, DMARC and alignment, with one-click unsubscribe for marketing and subscribed mail. Use the current provider guidance for scope; do not change DMARC enforcement before accounting for legitimate senders.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Test unsubscribe as a complete path</h2>
      <p>Inspect the unsubscribe headers and the visible body link on the actual campaign template. Exercise both paths using a staff subscription. Confirm the resulting suppression reaches every promotional sender that should honor it, including scheduled jobs and external capture imports.</p>
      <p>Google requires spam rates in Postmaster Tools below 0.3%; that ceiling is not a target or a universal campaign pause threshold. Its guidance also covers DNS, TLS and message formatting. Follow the <a class="link" href="https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126">full requirements</a> rather than treating authentication alone as inbox assurance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Keep acquisition and permission evidence</h2>
      <p>For each capture source, retain the signup context, permission status and suppression history. Review giveaway imports, partner transfers and historical migrations separately. Quarantine records with unresolved provenance instead of treating an imported email address as permission for a new campaign.</p>
      <p>Confirmed signup can help verify control of an address, but does not replace appropriate permission records or guarantee placement. Do not infer one country’s consent rules from another. This workflow is an operational review, not a universal legal rule; route unresolved permission questions to the responsible reviewer before sending.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Use engagement signals with their limitations</h2>
      <p>Document the eligibility definition used by both marketing and operations. Separate recent clicks, orders, explicit preferences and opens. Privacy features and automated activity can distort opens or clicks, so absence of opens alone is not a complete account of customer interest.</p>
      <p>Set inactivity and re-entry policies around message expectations and available evidence. Suppression can preserve the audit record without continued promotional sending. Do not automatically re-import suppressed contacts during migration or send an annual reactivation blast simply because a calendar elapsed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Read delivery diagnostics at the right level</h2>
      <p>Separate attempted, accepted, deferred and rejected messages. Preserve SMTP response text and classify failures by provider, stream, domain, campaign and acquisition cohort. A temporary deferral differs from an invalid address; automatic repeated retries to a permanent failure are not a recovery strategy.</p>
      <p>Accepted delivery is not measured inbox placement. Seed observations are diagnostic samples, not a census of customer inboxes. Compare provider reputation data and complaint reports using their own denominators and reporting delays. Do not substitute total sends for the denominator of a mailbox-provider spam metric.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Contain an incident without losing the evidence</h2>
      <p>Record the first affected send, recent DNS or integration changes, audience source, volume pattern and response codes. Pause the implicated promotional queue or segment while investigating. Preserve needed order communication through a verified working path and escalate failures in essential messages to the appropriate owner.</p>
      <p>Check whether the cause is authentication, list provenance, suppression failure, unexpected volume or another delivery error before choosing a remedy. Do not use a domain switch or a whole-list “we are back” blast as a cure. Keep incident snapshots so a falling open rate is not automatically blamed on reputation when tracking changed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Resume against explicit criteria</h2>
      <p>Write the resolved cause, eligible audience, initial scope, monitoring signals and rollback action. Confirm authentication and suppression fixtures before restarting. Increase volume only after evaluating available delivery feedback, taking account of delayed reports and normal traffic. No fixed weekly percentage or elapsed pause guarantees recovery.</p>
      <p>For a platform migration, transfer suppressions and permission history before enabling sends. Compare candidate audiences without duplicate live messaging, pause the replaced stream, and retain exports needed for reconciliation. Peak-sale plans should fit this readiness decision; see <a class="link" href="/use-cases/black-friday">Black Friday operations</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Rehearse failures and review actual outcomes</h2>
      <p>Proposed rehearsal: use staff accounts to unsubscribe via both paths, then attempt a scheduled promotion and confirm suppression. Inspect samples from marketing and order streams after a DNS change. Feed a permanent rejection into the handling path and verify no inappropriate new campaign enrollment.</p>
      <p>Test a delayed report, a stale import and an old sender left active after migration. Record actual evidence and owner actions. Staff delivery and seed checks cannot establish production inbox performance or promise a recovery date. Repeat relevant checks after changes rather than prescribing an arbitrary quarterly ritual for every store.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Make support and cost assessable</h2>
      <p>Ask a candidate who owns DNS, IP-level issues, complaint handling, suppression exports and incident response. Request sample diagnostic exports and demonstrate the pause and resume procedure. Compare the actual support scope and retention of evidence; supplier ratings and unified-sending slogans cannot establish deliverability superiority.</p>
      <p>Estimate platform, monitoring, integration and operator costs against realistic send volume. Report incident-affected orders and support work where observable. Do not multiply an open-rate drop by assumed revenue per open to claim lost profit. Coordinate definitions with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a> and audience rules with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">segmentation</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Deliverability operations FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
